WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.533
You run your program, and the cat
spins when it should have jumped.

2
00:00:08.753 --> 00:00:13.351
That is a bug. Finding it and
fixing it is called debugging.

3
00:00:13.851 --> 00:00:18.866
Debugging is not luck. It is a
habit with five steps, every time.

4
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:22.616
Run it first. You cannot
fix what you have not seen.

5
00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:26.412
Look: where did it go wrong?
Think: which block did that?

6
00:00:26.632 --> 00:00:30.393
Fix that one block, not the
whole program. Then run again.

7
00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:34.282
It is easy to skip look and
change the first block you see.

8
00:00:34.502 --> 00:00:37.288
Watch where it went
wrong. The bug is there.

9
00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:42.200
The cat should shrink, then jump,
then hide. That is what you wanted.

10
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:48.550
Run it. The cat grows, jumps, and
then spins. Two blocks are wrong.

11
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.718
The first block grows. You wanted
shrink, so that block is the bug.

12
00:00:52.938 --> 00:00:57.256
Fix it to shrink and run again.
The cat still spins at the end.

13
00:00:57.476 --> 00:01:00.913
The second bug was hiding
behind the first one all along.

14
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:05.452
Change spin to hide, run again,
and the cat does what you wanted.

15
00:01:05.952 --> 00:01:10.317
Every programmer makes mistakes.
Good ones write them down as rules.

16
00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:15.088
The cat began in the wrong
place. The rule: reset first.

17
00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.884
The repeat repeated the wrong
block. Check the block after it.

18
00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:23.005
You fixed a bug and never ran
it. Run again after every fix.

19
00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:27.126
Your next program is better
because of the last one's mistakes.

20
00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:31.202
Programmers say bug when they
are talking to each other.

21
00:01:31.422 --> 00:01:35.787
Written down properly, a mistake
in a program is called an error.

22
00:01:36.007 --> 00:01:40.698
You report the error: what you
expected, and what actually happened.

23
00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:43.890
Then you debug it. The
two words go together.

24
00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:48.243
Every program ever written has
had errors. Somebody kept looking.

25
00:01:48.743 --> 00:01:52.783
Your partner did not write your
program, so they read every block.

26
00:01:53.003 --> 00:01:57.369
You skip the blocks you are sure
about, and one of those is the bug.

27
00:01:57.589 --> 00:02:00.375
Explain it to them out
loud, block by block.

28
00:02:00.595 --> 00:02:05.611
Halfway through you stop: it should
not spin. You found it yourself.

29
00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:09.964
One of you presses Run. The other
watches, and sees it go wrong.

30
00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:14.225
Debugging is a habit: run,
look, think, fix, run again.

31
00:02:14.445 --> 00:02:18.764
A bug is a mistake in a program.
Written down, it is an error.

32
00:02:18.984 --> 00:02:22.235
Every mistake gives you a
rule for your next program.

33
00:02:22.455 --> 00:02:26.774
And fresh eyes find the block
you keep skipping. Ask a partner.
